\subsection{The Fake Safety Problem}\label{sec:fake_safety}

At first glance, the collision avoidance results for SDF-augmented VLA models appear compelling. Our A1-LoRA model (Pi0 fine-tuned with an SDF auxiliary loss) achieves a Collision Avoidance Rate (CAR) of 91.5\% across 800 evaluation episodes on the SafeLIBERO benchmark~\cite{hu2025vlsa}, a figure that would suggest reliable obstacle avoidance. Yet this headline metric conceals a fundamental problem. When we examine the end-effector displacement of the 732 episodes classified as ``safe,'' the picture changes entirely: 541 episodes (73.9\%) exhibit a maximum displacement below 1\,$\mu$m, and another 188 episodes (25.7\%) remain below 0.1\,mm. Only 3 episodes (0.4\%) show displacement above the 0.1\,mm threshold, the minimum we consider consistent with intentional motion. The remaining 99.6\% of safe episodes are artifacts of a robot that never moved.

We define a safe episode as \emph{fake safe} when the maximum end-effector displacement stays below a threshold $\tau_d = 0.1$\,mm. Under this criterion, 729 of the 732 safe episodes produced by A1-LoRA are fake safe. The robot did not avoid obstacles; it avoided motion altogether. The displacement distribution, shown in Figure~\ref{fig:displacement}, clusters overwhelmingly near zero, with no meaningful spread toward the range that would indicate planned trajectories around obstacles. Table~\ref{tab:fake_safety} summarizes the fake safety statistics across all evaluated methods.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Fake safety decomposition across methods on SafeLIBERO. CAR$_\text{f}$ excludes episodes with max displacement $< \tau_d = 0.1$\,mm. Fake safe rate is computed over safe (non-collision) episodes only.}
\label{tab:fake_safety}
\vspace{2pt}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
Method & CAR (\%) & TSR (\%) & Fake Safe (\%) & Real Safe & CAR$_\text{f}$ (\%) \\
\midrule
A1-LoRA (SDF) & 91.5 & 0.0 & 99.6 & 3 / 732 & ---$^\dagger$ \\
LoRA baseline  & 82.0 & 0.0 & 99.4 & $\approx$\,1 / 164 & $\approx$\,0 \\
Pi0.5 (3-seed) & 26.8\,$\pm$\,1.6 & 74.7\,$\pm$\,1.5 & 98.2 & 1 / 56 & $\approx$\,0 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\vspace{2pt}
{\footnotesize $^\dagger$ Denominator $< 5$; statistically unreliable.}
\end{table}

This phenomenon is not an artifact of the SDF auxiliary loss. The LoRA baseline, trained without any collision avoidance objective, reports a CAR of 82.0\% with a Task Success Rate (TSR) of 0\%. Among its 164 safe episodes, 99.4\% are fake safe by the same displacement criterion. The pattern persists even in Pi0.5, a larger, more capable model from the same family (2.5B$\to$3.5B parameters, extended training data). Across three seeds (600 total episodes), Pi0.5 achieves a CAR of 26.8\,$\pm$\,1.6\% and a TSR of 74.7\,$\pm$\,1.5\%. Its lower CAR reflects the fact that Pi0.5 actually moves the robot, which naturally increases collision frequency. Yet among its safe episodes, the fake safe rate remains 98.2\% (55 of 56 safe episodes in seed 0 show near-zero displacement). The conclusion is consistent across methods: when a Pi0-family model does not collide, it is almost certainly because it did not move.

These observations motivate a corrected metric. We define the \emph{displacement-filtered CAR} (CAR$_\text{f}$) as the fraction of safe episodes among only those episodes where the robot exhibits meaningful motion (max displacement $\geq \tau_d$). Formally:
\begin{equation}
\text{CAR}_\text{f} = \frac{|\{e : \text{safe}(e) \land d_{\max}(e) \geq \tau_d\}|}{|\{e : d_{\max}(e) \geq \tau_d\}|}
\end{equation}
where $d_{\max}(e)$ denotes the maximum end-effector displacement in episode $e$. We provide a sensitivity analysis over $\tau_d \in \{0.01, 0.1, 1, 10\}$\,mm in Appendix~\ref{app:threshold_sensitivity}, showing that conclusions are robust to threshold choice. For A1-LoRA, only 3 of 800 episodes exceed the displacement threshold, all 3 of which happen to be safe, but the denominator is too small to constitute a meaningful rate. For the LoRA baseline and Pi0.5, CAR$_\text{f}$ is similarly near zero. The gap between raw CAR and CAR$_\text{f}$ exposes the scale of contamination: a model can report over 90\% CAR while possessing essentially no collision avoidance capability.

The practical implication is stark. Standard CAR evaluation conflates two behaviors that differ in every meaningful respect: a robot that plans a safe trajectory around an obstacle, and a robot that has collapsed into inaction due to distributional shift. Without displacement filtering, any model suffering from action collapse will receive inflated safety scores, and models that collapse more severely will paradoxically appear safer. This inversion renders CAR, as conventionally computed, unreliable for evaluating collision avoidance in VLA models.

\section{Experimental Framework}\label{sec:framework}

This section defines the evaluation platform, metrics, and model configurations that underpin the analyses in Sections~\ref{sec:fake_safety}--\ref{sec:a1_pi05}. Our primary methodological contribution is the displacement-filtered Collision Avoidance Rate (CAR$_\text{f}$), which separates genuine avoidance from action collapse.

\subsection{Benchmark and Evaluation Protocol}\label{sec:benchmark}

We conduct all experiments on SafeLIBERO~\cite{hu2025vlsa}, a collision-aware evaluation extension of the LIBERO simulation platform~\cite{liu2023libero}. SafeLIBERO augments standard manipulation benchmarks with per-timestep collision detection and non-target obstacle annotations, enabling systematic measurement of both task completion and safety.

We select the \textit{object\_pudding} Level~I suite, which comprises four tabletop manipulation tasks. In each task, a target object is surrounded by non-target obstacles that the robot must avoid during grasping and transport. The MuJoCo physics engine provides collision detection: any contact between the robot (end-effector or arm links) and a non-target object registers as a collision event.

Each method is evaluated for 50 episodes per task (200 episodes total). For multi-seed experiments (Pi0.5), we run 3 seeds to produce 600 episodes per configuration. Initial object configurations are fixed across methods to ensure fair comparison.

\subsection{Evaluation Metrics}\label{sec:metrics}

\paragraph{Standard metrics.} We report two conventional metrics. The \emph{Task Success Rate} (TSR) is the fraction of episodes where the manipulation task reaches completion. The \emph{Collision Avoidance Rate} (CAR) is the fraction of episodes in which no collision occurs between the robot and any non-target object.

\paragraph{Displacement-filtered CAR.} Standard CAR conflates two qualitatively distinct behaviors: a robot that plans a trajectory around obstacles, and a robot that has collapsed into inaction. Both register as ``safe,'' yet only the former reflects collision avoidance capability. We propose the displacement-filtered CAR (CAR$_\text{f}$), which excludes episodes exhibiting action collapse from both numerator and denominator.

We define an episode $e$ as \emph{action-collapsed} when its maximum end-effector displacement satisfies $d_{\max}(e) < \tau_d$, where $\tau_d = 0.1$\,mm. The filtered metric is then:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:car_f}
\text{CAR}_\text{f} = \frac{|\{e : \text{safe}(e) \;\land\; d_{\max}(e) \geq \tau_d\}|}{|\{e : d_{\max}(e) \geq \tau_d\}|}
\end{equation}
where $d_{\max}(e)$ denotes the maximum Euclidean displacement of the end-effector across all timesteps in episode $e$. The threshold $\tau_d = 0.1$\,mm corresponds to sub-actuator-resolution motion and is robust to perturbation (Appendix~\ref{app:threshold_sensitivity}).

\paragraph{Auxiliary metrics.} We additionally report the \emph{Frozen Rate}, the fraction of episodes with $d_{\max} < \tau_d$, which quantifies the severity of action collapse for each method. The \emph{Simultaneous Success Rate} (SSR $= $ TSR $\cap$ CAR) measures the fraction of episodes that achieve both task completion and collision avoidance.

\subsection{Models and Training}\label{sec:models}

\paragraph{Base architectures.} We evaluate two VLA models from the Pi0 family~\cite{black2024pi0}. \textbf{Pi0} (${\sim}$3B parameters) uses a PaliGemma vision-language encoder paired with a dedicated action expert that generates continuous actions via flow matching~\cite{lipman2023flow}. \textbf{Pi0.5} extends this architecture to ${\sim}$3.5B parameters with broader pre-training data, and serves as a stronger baseline that exhibits less action collapse.

\paragraph{Training configurations.} We evaluate four model variants:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textit{Full-FT baseline}: Pi0 with all parameters fine-tuned on SafeLIBERO demonstrations using the flow-matching action loss.
\item \textit{LoRA baseline}: Pi0 with LoRA adapters (${\sim}$33.5M trainable parameters), same demonstrations and loss.
\item \textit{A1-LoRA}: Pi0 + LoRA augmented with two auxiliary heads. An SDF Decoder (4-layer MLP, ${\sim}$395K parameters) predicts the signed distance from the end-effector to the nearest obstacle surface, supervised with a clamped $L_1$ loss and an Eikonal regularizer ($\mathcal{L}_\text{SDF}$). An EE Extractor (2-layer MLP, ${\sim}$263K parameters) predicts end-effector position from action expert features ($\mathcal{L}_\text{EE}$, MSE loss). SDF ground truth is computed from known obstacle meshes in simulation.
\item \textit{Pi0.5 + A1}: The A1 architecture applied to the Pi0.5 backbone. Training proceeds in two phases: Phase~1 (steps 0--5K) trains only the auxiliary heads on detached features with the backbone frozen; Phase~2 (steps 5K--30K) unfreezes LoRA parameters for joint optimization. We evaluate $\lambda_\text{SDF} \in \{0.005, 0.01\}$.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Oracle SDF Refinement}\label{sec:oracle_setup}

To test the theoretical upper bound of end-effector-only SDF refinement (analyzed in Section~\ref{sec:ee_only}), we replace the learned SDF network with the ground-truth signed distance field computed from simulation geometry. This eliminates prediction error and isolates the structural limitation of the refinement paradigm itself.

We evaluate three oracle variants that differ in how the SDF gradient is applied:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textit{Oracle-adaptive}: runtime gradient refinement with a dynamic step size $\alpha$ that scales inversely with gradient magnitude.
\item \textit{Oracle-fixed}: a constant step size $\alpha = 0.005$.
\item \textit{Oracle-feature}: gradient applied in the VLA's latent feature space rather than Cartesian action space.
\end{itemize}
Each variant is evaluated for 160 episodes (4 tasks $\times$ 40 episodes) with a single seed.

\subsection{SDF Ground Truth}\label{sec:sdf_gt}

The workspace signed distance field is computed from the known obstacle meshes available in the LIBERO/MuJoCo simulation environment. SDF values are positive in free space, zero on the obstacle surface, and negative inside the obstacle volume. Ground-truth labels are generated at query points sampled along predicted end-effector trajectories during training.
